      ":question/id": "d6cd4615-1763-53c3-a270-8e9c10488c8f",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{6}{x}y = 3, x > 0$.\n\nSelect the correct options below:\n\nAn integrating factor for this equation is $I(x) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe general solution is given by $y(x) =$ {{field-2}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{6}{x}$ and $Q(x)=3.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (6/x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{6\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{6})} \\\\  & ={x}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={x}^{6}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{6}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{6}{x}y) & ={x}^{6}\\cdot 3 \\\\ {x}^{6}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6{x}^{5}y & =3{x}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(y{x}^{6})\\,dx & =\\int 3{x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{6} & =\\frac{3{x}^{7}}{7}+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{3x}{7}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
